{s:2,k:'notebook',term:'Key ideas',title:'The chain set in four lines',say:`Four lines on the chain set. V one is [A](/ˈA/) V two minus B I two; the minus sign belongs to the definition, however lonely it looks. Open port two for [A](/ˈA/) and C; short it for B and D. Series and shunt arms have simple blocks. And a cascade is a product, source side first.`,cues:{i1:'Four lines on',i1b:'the minus sign',i2:'Open port two',i2b:'short it for',i3:'Series and shunt',i4:'And a cascade'},items:[['V₁ = AV₂ − BI₂,  I₁ = CV₂ − DI₂','i1'],['Port 2 open: A and C.  Shorted: B and D','i2'],['Series Z: [1 Z; 0 1].  Shunt Y: [1 0; Y 1]','i3'],['Cascade: [T] = [T₁][T₂], source side first','i4']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'i1'),CT_box(14,10,612,150)+CT_T(34,44,'1 · the current leaving port 2',{fs:24,fw:700})+CT_2p(190,70,140,84,'T',{L:60})+CT_arrow(136,88,184,88,GRN,4)+CT_T(152,76,CT_sub('I','1'),{fs:24,fw:800,fill:GRN})+CT_L(390,88,480,88,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(390,136,480,136,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(480,88,480,95,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(480,129,480,136,CT_INK,3)+CT_box(468,95,24,34,{r:4,st:ORG,sw:3})+CT_T(504,120,'load',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:ORG}))}
${g('rise',tk(c,'i1b'),CT_arrow(398,88,458,88,ORG,5)+CT_T(428,76,'−I₂',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i2'),CT_box(14,170,612,160)+CT_T(34,204,'2 · the two tests at port 2',{fs:24,fw:700})+CT_2p(90,222,110,70,'T',{L:30,fs:22})+CT_T(145,318,'open: A, C',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i2b'),CT_2p(390,222,110,70,'T',{L:30,fs:22})+CT_L(530,240,530,274,GRN,6)+CT_T(445,318,'shorted: B, D',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i3'),CT_box(14,340,612,190)+CT_T(34,374,'3 · two building blocks',{fs:24,fw:700})+CT_res(34,190,400,BLU)+CT_L(34,510,190,510,CT_INK,3)+CT_term(34,400)+CT_term(190,400)+CT_term(34,510)+CT_term(190,510)+CT_T(112,440,'Z',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU,it:1})+CT_mat(252,455,[['1','Z'],['0','1']],{fs:24,cw:44})
 +CT_L(322,400,470,400,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(322,510,470,510,CT_INK,3)+CT_resV(396,400,510,GRN)+CT_dot(396,400)+CT_dot(396,510)+CT_term(322,400)+CT_term(470,400)+CT_term(322,510)+CT_term(470,510)+CT_T(416,462,'Y',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:GRN,it:1})+CT_mat(554,455,[['1','0'],['Y','1']],{fs:24,cw:44}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i4'),CT_box(14,540,612,140)+CT_T(34,574,'4 · a cascade multiplies',{fs:24,fw:700})+CT_2p(110,590,100,66,'T₁',{L:26,fs:24})+CT_L(236,608,278,608,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(236,638,278,638,CT_INK,3)+CT_2p(304,590,100,66,'T₂',{L:26,fs:24}))}
${g('rise',tk(c,['i4',1.4]),CT_T(604,632,'[T] = [T₁][T₂]',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU}))}`},
/* book Worked example (chapter 6.4), steps 1 to 5 */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3",title:"Worked example 3",say:`Example three, the same T network. Open port two: V one is six I one, V two is four I one. So [A](/ˈA/) is one point five, and C is a quarter siemens. Short it: B is six point five ohms, and D is one point seven five. Check: [A](/ˈA/) D minus B C is exactly one.`,pause:.8,cues:{fk:'Example three',k1:'Open port two',k1b:'is one point five',k2:'Short it',k2b:'and D is',k3:'Check',k3b:'is exactly one',a:'is exactly one'},q:['The same T network: Z₁ = 2 Ω, Z₂ = 3 Ω, Z₃ = 4 Ω.','Find A, B, C, D from tests at port 2; check AD − BC.'],f:['A = V₁/V₂,  C = I₁/V₂   (I₂ = 0)','B = V₁/(−I₂),  D = I₁/(−I₂)   (V₂ = 0)'],hint:'Open port 2 for A and C; short it for B and D.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Port 2 open", ["V₁ = 6I₁,  V₂ = 4I₁","A = 6/4 = 1.5,  C = 1/4 = 0.25 S"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Port 2 shorted", ["V₁ = (26/7)I₁,  −I₂ = (4/7)I₁","B = 26/4 = 6.5 Ω,  D = 7/4 = 1.75"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Check", ["AD − BC = 1.5 × 1.75 − 6.5 × 0.25","AD − BC = 2.625 − 1.625 = 1"], ["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2],ans:['A = 1.5, B = 6.5 Ω, C = 0.25 S, D = 1.75','a']},
/* book Worked example 2 (chapter 6.4), steps 1 to 6 */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 4",title:"Worked example 4",say:'Example four builds it from three blocks. The first two multiply to one point five, two, a quarter, one. Times the three ohm block: one point five, six point five, a quarter, one point seven five. The same matrix.',cues:{fk:'Example four builds',k1:'The first two',k1b:'multiply to one',k2:'Times the three',k2b:'The same matrix'},q:['Cascade: series 2 Ω, shunt 0.25 S, series 3 Ω,','then a 10 Ω load. Find [T], Zin and V₂/V₁.'],f:['[T] = [T₁][T₂][T₃]',`${CT_sub('Z','in')} = (A${CT_sub('Z','L')} + B)/(C${CT_sub('Z','L')} + D)`],hint:'Series Z: [1 Z; 0 1].  Shunt Y: [1 0; Y 1].',fk:'fk',steps:[["First two", ["[1 2; 0 1] × [1 0; 0.25 1]","[T₁][T₂] = [1.5 2; 0.25 1]"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Times the third", ["[1.5 2; 0.25 1] × [1 3; 0 1]","[T] = [1.5 6.5; 0.25 1.75] ✓"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Input impedance", ["Zin = (1.5 × 10 + 6.5)/(0.25 × 10 + 1.75)",`${CT_sub('Z','in')} = 21.5/4.25 = 5.06 Ω`], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Check and gain", ["4 ∥ (3 + 10) = 3.06 Ω,  + 2 = 5.06 Ω ✓","5.06 Ω ✓;  V₂/V₁ = 10/21.5 = 0.465"], ["k4","k4b"]]],live:[0,1]},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 4 (load)",title:"Worked example 4",say:'Add a ten ohm load. The input impedance is twenty-one point five over four point two five: five point zero six ohms. Series-parallel agrees. The voltage gain: ten over twenty-one point five, zero point four six five.',pause:1,cues:{fk:'Add a ten',k3:'The input impedance',k3b:'five point zero',k4:'Series-parallel agrees',k4b:'The voltage gain',a:'zero point four'},q:['Cascade: series 2 Ω, shunt 0.25 S, series 3 Ω,','then a 10 Ω load. Find [T], Zin and V₂/V₁.'],f:['[T] = [T₁][T₂][T₃]',`${CT_sub('Z','in')} = (A${CT_sub('Z','L')} + B)/(C${CT_sub('Z','L')} + D)`],hint:'Series Z: [1 Z; 0 1].  Shunt Y: [1 0; Y 1].',fk:'fk',steps:[["First two", ["[1 2; 0 1] × [1 0; 0.25 1]","[T₁][T₂] = [1.5 2; 0.25 1]"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Times the third", ["[1.5 2; 0.25 1] × [1 3; 0 1]","[T] = [1.5 6.5; 0.25 1.75] ✓"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Input impedance", ["Zin = (1.5 × 10 + 6.5)/(0.25 × 10 + 1.75)",`${CT_sub('Z','in')} = 21.5/4.25 = 5.06 Ω`], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Check and gain", ["4 ∥ (3 + 10) = 3.06 Ω,  + 2 = 5.06 Ω ✓","5.06 Ω ✓;  V₂/V₁ = 10/21.5 = 0.465"], ["k4","k4b"]]],live:[2,3],ans:['Zin = 5.06 Ω, V₂/V₁ = 0.465','a']},
{s:2,k:'trap2',term:'Exam trap',title:'Exam trap!',say:'The second trap is order. Multiply the blocks load end first, and you get one point seven five, six point five, a quarter, one point five: the network turned round. Write them in signal order, source side on the left.',cues:{x:'The second trap',w:'Multiply the blocks',r:'Write them in'},wl:"Don't write",rl:'Write',wrong:['[T] = [T₃][T₂][T₁]  (load side first)','= [1.75 6.5; 0.25 1.5]'],right:['[T] = [T₁][T₂][T₃]  (source side first)','= [1.5 6.5; 0.25 1.75]'],wv:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_ladder(['3 Ω','0.25 S','2 Ω'],TRED)}${CT_T(300,312,'load side first: the network turned round',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED})}`,rv:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_ladder(['2 Ω','0.25 S','3 Ω'],GRN)}${CT_arrow(110,20,400,20,GRN,3)}${CT_T(440,28,'signal',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN})}${CT_T(300,312,'source side on the left: [T₁][T₂][T₃]',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})}`},
/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Recap in three. The h set takes I one and V two, and models a transistor. The chain set takes V two and minus I two. And a cascade multiplies matrices, source side first.',cues:{r1:'The h set',r2:'The chain set',r3:'And a cascade'},panels:[["Hybrid (h)","BLU",'ctHm',['V₁ = h₁₁I₁ + h₁₂V₂','I₂ = h₂₁I₁ + h₂₂V₂'],'r1'],["Transmission","ORG",'ctChainI',['V₁ = AV₂ − BI₂','I₁ = CV₂ − DI₂'],'r2'],["Cascade","GRN",'ctCascade',['[T] = [T₁][T₂][T₃]','source side on the left'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the output current written as −I₂ in the chain equations?",say:'Question one. Why is the output current written as minus I two, in the chain equations?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why is the'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the output current written as −I₂ in the chain equations?",say:'I two is defined entering port two, but in a chain the current that matters leaves it, into the next stage. Writing minus I two makes each output the next input, so cascades simply multiply.',cues:{a1:'I two is',a2:'Writing minus I'},al:[["I₂ is defined entering port 2; in a chain, the current that matters leaves port 2.",'a1'],["Writing −I₂ makes one stage's output vector the next one's input, so cascades multiply.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_2p(60,120,150,120,'a',{L:40,fs:34})+CT_arrow(262,168,220,168,ORG,4)+CT_T(24,90,`${CT_sub('I','2')} of stage a: defined entering`,{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_2p(370,120,150,120,'b',{L:40,fs:34})+CT_L(250,138,330,138,GRN,5)+CT_L(250,222,330,222,GRN,5)+CT_arrow(258,198,326,198,GRN,4)+CT_T(310,320,`−${CT_sub('I','2a')} = ${CT_sub('I','1b')}`,{fs:30,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})+CT_T(310,374,`${CT_sub('V','2a')} = ${CT_sub('V','1b')}`,{fs:30,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+CT_T(310,440,`[T] = [${CT_sub('T','a')}][${CT_sub('T','b')}]`,{fs:32,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:CT_INK}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A two-port has h₁₁ = 2 kΩ, h₁₂ = 0, h₂₁ = 100, h₂₂ = 50 μS. With I₁ = 20 μA and V₂ = 0, find V₁ and I₂.",say:'Question two. A two-port has h one one, two kilohms; h one two, zero; h two one, one hundred; h two two, fifty microsiemens. With I one twenty microamps and V two zero, find V one and I two.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'A two-port has'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A two-port has h₁₁ = 2 kΩ, h₁₂ = 0, h₂₁ = 100, h₂₂ = 50 μS. With I₁ = 20 μA and V₂ = 0, find V₁ and I₂.",say:'With V two zero, the h one two and h two two terms vanish. V one is two thousand times twenty microamps: zero point zero four volts. I two is one hundred times twenty microamps: two milliamps.',cues:{fk:'With V two',k1:'the h one two',k2:'V one is',k2b:'zero point zero four',k3:'I two is',k3b:'two milliamps'},calc:{f:['V₁ = h₁₁I₁ + h₁₂V₂','I₂ = h₂₁I₁ + h₂₂V₂'],hint:'20 μA = 20 × 10⁻⁶ A; 2 kΩ = 2000 Ω.',fk:'fk',steps:[["V₂ = 0","h₁₂V₂ = 0  and  h₂₂V₂ = 0","k1"],["Input",["V₁ = 2000 × 20 × 10⁻⁶","V₁ = 0.04 V"],["k2","k2b"]],["Output",["I₂ = 100 × 20 × 10⁻⁶","I₂ = 2 × 10⁻³ A = 2 mA"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A student measures h₁₂ with the output shorted and reads zero. What went wrong?",say:'Question three. A student measures h one two with the output shorted, and reads zero. What went wrong?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'A student measures'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A student measures h₁₂ with the output shorted and reads zero. What went wrong?",say:'h one two is V one over V two: it needs the input open and port two driven. With the output shorted, V two is zero, so the ratio means nothing. Open port one, apply V two, and measure the voltage there.',cues:{a1:'h one two is',a2:'With the output',a3:'Open port one'},al:[["h₁₂ = V₁/V₂ needs the input open and port 2 driven.",'a1'],["With the output shorted V₂ = 0, so the ratio has no meaning.",'a2'],["Open port 1, apply V₂ and measure the voltage at port 1.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_T(310,40,'right: input open, port 2 driven',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})+CT_2p(220,70,160,110,'h',{L:60})+CT_L(440,88,500,88,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(440,162,500,162,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(500,88,500,99,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(500,151,500,162,CT_INK,3)+CT_vs(500,125,26)+CT_T(540,134,CT_sub('V','2'),{fs:26,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_T(310,282,'wrong: output shorted',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED})+CT_2p(220,310,160,110,'h',{L:60})+CT_L(440,328,440,402,TRED,6)+CT_T(310,474,`${CT_sub('V','2')} = 0, so ${CT_sub('V','1')}/${CT_sub('V','2')} = ?`,{fs:28,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),CT_L(160,88,100,88,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(160,162,100,162,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(100,88,100,99,CT_INK,3)+CT_L(100,151,100,162,CT_INK,3)+`<circle cx="100" cy="125" r="26" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="${BLU}" stroke-width="4"/>`+CT_T(100,134,'V',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+CT_T(310,224,`measure ${CT_sub('V','1')}:  ${CT_sub('h','12')} = ${CT_sub('V','1')}/${CT_sub('V','2')}`,{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the h-parameter model and the three-block cascade from memory, then check them.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the h'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["In this lesson", "Hybrid (h) and inverse hybrid (g) parameters; Transmission (ABCD) parameters"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The h-parameter model", "h₁₁ in series with h₁₂V₂ at the input; h₂₁I₁ beside 1/h₂₂ at the output. The example gives V₁ = 0 V and I₂ = 0.4 mA."], ["The T network as a cascade", "Series 2 Ω, shunt 0.25 S, series 3 Ω multiply to [1.5 6.5; 0.25 1.75]; with a 10 Ω load, Zin = 5.06 Ω."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: M. E. Van Valkenburg (Network Analysis); William H. Hayt, Jack E. Kemmerly and Steven M. Durbin (Engineering Circuit Analysis); Franklin F. Kuo (Network Analysis and Synthesis)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: h mixes its units, the chain set keeps its minus sign, and a cascade multiplies in order. Next time, relationships and conversions between parameter sets. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'h mixes its',w2:'the chain set',w3:'and a cascade',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['h: MIXED UNITS','BLU','w1'],['KEEP THE −I₂','ORG','w2'],['MULTIPLY IN ORDER','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Relationships and conversions between parameter sets · Interconnection of two-ports", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
